WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.029
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Dr. Ashish Jha, who is a physician,

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:11.070
Brown University professor and a world renowned
expert on pandemic preparedness and response.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.620
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.500
I have so many questions and we'll just jump
right in.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.850
First things first.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:26.000
Lots of folks who were very confident in following
CDC guidelines for 95 percent of this pandemic

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.450
were taking a little bit of back at what seemed
like a very rapid advancement from the CDC

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:36.000
in terms of mask wearing and what's safe,
particularly for vaccinated people.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.460
What what's your general sense of the balance
that the CDC struck between what makes sense

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.870
medically and also trying to do things to
encourage more people to want to get vaccinated?

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.030
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.720
So first of all, thanks for having me here.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:48.870
I'm excited to be on.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.079
And, you know, the CDC has a couple of different
jobs, right?

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:56.220
On one hand, they want to explain to people
what the science is.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.829
They want to explain to people what the evidence
is.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:00.380
So you have to go read all the scientific
papers yourself.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.680
You can if you want, but you don't have to.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.970
The CDC will give you that summary.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:09.850
And then they also have a job of giving advice
to policymakers about how to manage that science.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.540
And what the CDC did was they came out and
said, look, the science is pretty clear.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.979
If you've been fully vaccinated, you're good
to go.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:20.889
You do not need to be wearing a mask in most
instances.

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:22.310
And you're really you're fine.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:28.310
The problem is that implementing that policy
is hard for governors because they don't know

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.680
who's vaccinated and who's not.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.490
And I think there was maybe a little more
attention could have been paid to thinking

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.009
through, well, how is this going to get implemented?

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:42.069
But the people who defend the CBC's decision
basically say mask mandates were kind of on

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:43.310
their way out anyway.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.039
A lot of states had already lifted them.

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:49.450
And so what the CDC was really doing was just
explaining to people what they already knew,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.409
which was that all the mask wearing, all the
mask mandates, they're kind of on their way

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:54.409
out.

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:59.270
Speaker 1: The one thing that I hear from
lots of parents, which they just don't seem

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.860
to have an answer for, and I don't either,
is the numbers are going down.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.739
We're seeing lower numbers in many states,
almost down to statistical baseline of risk

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:11.009
for being out in the world, et cetera.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.269
But nobody under 12 has been vaccinated.

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:19.000
And so how does this all compute and how are
we to to think about that part?

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.430
Speaker 2: Yeah, it's a really good question.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:23.390
I have a yes, I have three kids.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:28.580
Two of them are above the age of 12 or 14
and 16, and one of them is nine, who obviously

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.710
has not gotten vaccinated.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.090
So I think about this a lot from my own family.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.160
And here is how I would think about this.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.330
And this is what I've recommended to people.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.080
First, if you want to protect kids under 12,
the single best thing you can do is make sure

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.819
everybody around them is vaccinated, their
parents, their older siblings.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:50.269
That becomes critical like that sort of creates
a ring of protection against those kids.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:54.620
Second, you want to live in a community with
relatively high vaccination rates.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.939
And what that will do is make sure infection
numbers are really low.

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:01.040
And then for some high risk things, maybe
you want to pay a little extra attention.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.379
So when I think about it, you know, we have
not done indoor dining with my nine year old.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:11.209
We probably could infection numbers where
I live a really, really low, but I've done

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:15.049
indoor dining by myself because I'm fully
vaccinated.

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:19.239
And so what I have recommended is watch the
infection numbers as they get lower and lower.

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:23.909
You can do more and more indoors without masks
for kids, but maybe for some indoor things,

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:27.329
it's reasonable to still have the kids mask
up.

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:30.370
If you're going to be in a crowded indoor
space, let's say you're in a community where

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.230
not everybody or not a lot of people are vaccinated.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.930
That's where you want to be careful.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.749
But other than that, right now, I think we're
pretty in pretty good shape, even for younger

00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:39.950
kids who have not gotten vaccinated.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:47.319
Speaker 1: Should there be special guidelines
for folks who have been vaccinated but have

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:55.049
either some particular medical condition or
are immunocompromised or and and if so, for

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:58.609
now, at what point can that be eased off of?

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:01.060
Speaker 2: Yeah, it's a very good question.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.840
You know, so what's interesting about this,
and I get asked about this a lot from patients,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.690
and I talk about this a lot with my colleagues.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:14.750
Immunocompromised is a broad group of people
who have some amount of immunodeficiency.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.360
Some of those people, their vaccine works
just great.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.620
They're essentially it works as well as it
does for anybody else.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.430
Some of those other people, the vaccine barely
works at all.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.340
And so, first and foremost, you got to have
a talk with your doctor about the kind of

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.620
immediate compromise that you have, because
it really does vary a lot.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.950
Second, for the people who are unfortunately
in that small group for whom the vaccine essentially

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.180
doesn't work at all.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.540
There's studies going on of maybe a third
or fourth dose of the vaccine could get them

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:44.980
more protection.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.690
But unfortunately, they need to act like they're
unvaccinated because they are or their immune

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.480
system is acting that way.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.280
And so they need to continue to mask up.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.590
And if you're if you live with someone with
one of those more severe forms of immunocompromised,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.330
said, you have to be very careful.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.950
Certainly everybody around them should be
fully vaccinated.

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:07.901
One of the reasons why we've got to get to
very, very high levels of vaccination is there

00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:12.350
a small proportion of people in our country
who are vulnerable for whom vaccines won't

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:13.350
work.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.720
We've got to do a better job protecting them.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.440
Speaker 1: When we talk about some of the
variants, including the Delta variant, maybe

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.630
first to make sure I'm understanding the math
correctly.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:29.310
If we read, for example, that a particular
variant is 60 percent more transmissible,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:33.600
would it be correct to understand that that
means that given a particular scenario, call

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.650
it eight minutes indoors unvaccinated?

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.650
Right.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:43.430
If normally there would be a one percent transmission
risk for it to be 60 percent more transmissible,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.150
does that mean that that becomes a one point
six percent transmission risk?

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.130
Speaker 2: Yeah, exactly.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.480
Exactly that it's 60 percent relative.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.060
So you don't go from one to 60 one, thank
goodness.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.060
Of course.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.060
But right.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.420
If you see we're at 10 percent now you're
at 16 percent.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:05.010
It's that it's at 60 percent relative risk,
but may not sound like a lot, but it actually

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:08.230
when you apply it across the whole population,
it's massive.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:12.900
And you can see it in a place like the United
Kingdom, which, by the way, has higher vaccination

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.130
rates than we do still.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.290
I mean, we're about the same, but they're
a little higher.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:21.440
You know, they have seen because of the Delta
variant and the contagiousness, they have

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.670
seen a three to four fold increase in total
number of infections across the country despite

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.860
high vaccinations, because this this virus,
this variant is so much more contagious.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:37.640
Speaker 1: So for the average person who's
vaccinated, would learning about the increasing

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:42.330
prevalence of that variant change what one
considers to be safe?

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:43.330
Like what?

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.690
What I mean is if you're vaccinated and you
see the case numbers and you're saying I'm

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:51.930
going to do a trip in two weeks, we now learn
about the fact that this variant is out there.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.700
Should the average person change their activity
as a result of that?

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.190
Speaker 2: If you're fully vaccinated, not
if you're fully vaccinated.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:03.210
The good news is that the vaccines we have
hold up very, very well against the Delta

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.280
variant.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.360
So if you're let's say you're planning a trip
with four of your friends and they're all

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.360
fully vaccinated.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.160
Do your trip.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.500
Don't worry about the delta bearing the Delta
variant, you know, it poses a marginal problem

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.310
for people who are fully vaccinated, not much
of one.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:21.390
You just tend to get a little bit more through
infections, but not people don't get sick

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.410
because they're fully vaccinated.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.310
Delta variant is a huge problem for people
who are not vaccinated.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:32.300
Those people are at much, much higher risk
without, you know, with the delta than without.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.470
But if you're fully vaccinated, I wouldn't
worry.

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:40.220
Speaker 1: Can you talk a little bit about,
particularly in the last three weeks, the

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:45.770
hypotheses and discussions about the origins
of this virus have gone?

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:50.250
I don't want to say that it's gone mainstream,
but there's been significantly more discussion,

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.740
including in places where we were not originally
seeing it.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:58.830
My understanding of the spectrum of what is
possible would be on the one hand, this had

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:04.890
nothing to do with any lab, even as a pit
stop all the way to this was designed as a

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.820
bioweapon and deliberately released.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:13.720
I think it's important, no matter what we
explore, to not claim something to be likely

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.280
until we have reason to believe that it's
likely.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:19.830
But what do you make of the change in that
discussion over the last few weeks and sort

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:20.990
of the state of that question?

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.110
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:25.390
So let's talk about the science and where
we are first on the science, and then I would

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:26.900
talk about the discussion.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:27.900
Yeah.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.540
So you actually really laid out the spectrum?

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:35.630
Well, I could have just been a standard animal
to human species, jump very calm.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.550
And it's how we get Ebola.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:38.820
It's how we got Zika.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:43.130
This is pretty much the most common way that
we get novel infections all the way to.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:49.380
It was a bioweapon designed to cause a global
epidemic.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:55.250
But that so basically the way I look at it,
bioweapon, incredibly unlikely.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:09:00.710
A bit more likely is it was it was in a lab
now could in a naturally occurring virus.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.200
People were studying in the lab and there
was an accidental leak that is more likely

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.380
or it wasn't deliberately designed as a bioweapon,
but they were working on getting a function

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.000
and they caused a virus to become more lethal.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.320
And then it leaked, you know, a little bit
less likely, but still on the possibility

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:23.420
range, most likely just based on kind of experience,
historical, that there was an animal to human

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:28.620
jump, that there's a species of animals, probably
bats, maybe there's a different intermediary

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.760
and humans got infected.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.160
The reason I say that's most likely because,
again, that's how it usually happens.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.350
But here's the bottom line.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.200
I don't know the answer.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.600
And I would argue that probably no one else
does either.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:44.780
I mean, if it was a lab like maybe a small
number of people do, but I certainly don't.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:45.950
No one else in the United States does.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:46.960
So what do we need to do?

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.050
And first of all, why is it important?

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:49.050
It is important.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:50.480
We want to know where this virus came from.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.770
We want to understand it not just because
it helps us manage this one, but prevent future

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:55.770
ones.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:01.440
But the other part is we have got to stop
politicizing this like, my God, at this point,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.940
we have managed to take this thing.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.180
That's a very complicated and important scientific
question.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.310
And people are talking about it as though,
like, if you're a Democrat, you believe one

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.580
thing and a Republican, you believe another.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.250
I just don't know the right answer.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:14.250
We need to investigate it.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:17.140
We need to push China to give us more cooperation.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:21.220
We need to have the U.S. intelligence and
other intelligence agencies invested in it.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.350
We really do need to get to the bottom bottom
of this.

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.900
Again, if I were a betting person, I'd say
probably came from an animal.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:28.330
But we're not we're not making bets here.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:29.550
We should actually sort it out.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:36.490
Speaker 1: Is it the is it concerning or is
it in any way relevant or something we should

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:37.960
be thinking about that.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:45.240
It's not clear who the first human to be infected
was or is that just not particularly surprising

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.290
if indeed the first human or humans were in
a country of one point three billion people?

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:54.600
Speaker 2: Yeah, I think it's important to
try to figure out how far back does this go.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.650
So there's some recent data now that's coming
out that suggests that the first cases in

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:05.170
the United States may have been in late December
and then people who were not traveling, that

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:06.170
means that they got it.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:11.690
They were infected by somebody who had traveled
here maybe in early December pushing the timeline

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:12.690
back.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.970
You know, we probably won't find what people
talk about is kind of patient zero the first

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:16.970
person infected?

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:20.410
Yeah, I doubt we'll find that we might, but
I doubt it.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:24.640
But understanding when the virus got going,
when were the first group of people infected

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.320
who were the first group?

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:27.320
Were they people in the lab?

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.340
Were the people who shopped at that market?

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:31.840
I think that will be important.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:33.050
Part of the tracing here.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.860
This has been such a horrible pandemic.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.540
We have got to get to the best of our ability
to the origin, not just where it came from,

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.030
but who are the first people infected.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:45.620
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to ask about,
certainly you, like many of us, have been

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:51.060
seeing that there is a very significant correlation
right now between political views and and

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:56.290
vaccination rates, red and blue states, and
also within those states, individuals who

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:58.920
identify politically one way versus the other.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:05.140
Are these rates now or could they be different
enough that the future of this virus becomes

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:08.730
essentially a partizan virus where there is
one person?

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:12.390
Critical group that is disproportionately
dying from this in the United States.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.070
Speaker 2: He has a good question.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:16.890
So I actually see it a little bit differently.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:21.190
And let me kind of make the case for why,
OK, when I look at the data, I mean, there's

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.240
no doubt about it that if you just look red
states versus blue states, it looks like blue

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.540
states are doing great on vaccinations.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:28.230
Red states are not.

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:30.440
But there's other things about red states
versus blue states.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:34.529
So, for instance, public health infrastructure
or public health infrastructure is much better.

00:12:34.529 --> 00:12:38.400
And a lot of these, quote unquote blue states,
and that's what's allowing vaccines to get

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:39.400
out to people.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:41.690
I still find in a lot of, quote unquote, red
states.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:46.480
There are a lot of people who want to be vaccinated,
but they're far away from vaccine sites.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.460
It's not easy for them to get time off.

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:51.490
There are enough barriers that still preventing
people.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:55.440
So I hope that the partizan divide gets better.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.990
And part of it is I can look at states like
South Dakota, which is a very red state, and

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:04.589
sixty to sixty three percent of adults have
gotten vaccinated almost at the same level

00:13:04.589 --> 00:13:05.589
as the national number.

00:13:05.589 --> 00:13:09.190
And I think I feel like there are plenty of
people on both sides of the political aisle

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.740
who have gotten vaccinated.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:16.779
I don't I think if this were to become a partizan
virus, I think if it were to become something

00:13:16.779 --> 00:13:20.490
that infected one political group more than
I think it'd be very bad for our country,

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:26.290
it would really sap our desire to have kind
of a unified response.

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:28.340
It would make this just a bad thing.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.410
The virus certainly doesn't care about the
politics.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:35.790
